<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottoman–Habsburg wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1526">1526</a>–<a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ottoman-Habsburg rivalry · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ottoman–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> wars were a long series of conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the Habsburg monarchy, fought from the sixteenth to the eighteenth century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. They included the sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529 and 1683. The rivalry structured European geopolitics and alliances from the 1520s through the late seventeenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottoman-habsburg-wars.jpg" alt="Ottoman–Habsburg wars" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elanoraga · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MixCollage-15-Jan-2024-09-37-PM-7066.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> were occasionally kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and, almost always after the 15th century, emperors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but the earlier wars between Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> involved other dynasties too. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> saw the advancing Ottoman state as a threat to Christendom, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> of Nicopolis (1396) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> (1443–44) were the most determined attempts to halt it. For a time the Ottomans were occupied with Balkan rebels such as Vlad Dracula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>. When these and other rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states were defeated, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> lay open to invasion, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> bordered the Ottoman Empire and its vassals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> began with a decisive victory at Mohács and reduced about a third of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to tributary status. Sultan Suleiman regained most of Ferdinand's gains and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, after which Ferdinand recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> and other forts in 1530. At sea the Ottomans took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 1522, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1565 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1571, but suffered a decisive naval defeat at Lepanto.</p><p>In 1683 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> after fifteen months of mobilising. Sobieski and a coalition of Germans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> arrived as the defence was becoming untenable, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were defeated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a>, a large coalition, reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> in 1686 with more than 74,000 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, defeated the Ottomans at the Second Battle of Mohács in 1687.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the defeat at Zenta in 1697 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> sued for peace, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699 gave the Austrians the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. The Ottomans declared war again in 1716, but their army was broken and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> killed at Petrovaradin by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a>, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> a year later. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> in 1718 the Austrians gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeswar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>. The wars ended after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s participation in the war of 1787–1791, fought allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>Historians have long treated the second siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> as a decisive victory that saved Western civilisation. Recent historians take a broader view, noting that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> were at the same time resisting separatist movements and fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> kept military parity with the Habsburgs until the middle of the 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the Ottomans never fought again and were allied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottoman–Habsburg wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
